<!--
    Swift instantiation helpers and lazy witness table accessors named from
    their bodies (`option swifthelpers on|off`, the helper arm of
    `swiftmetadata`), and metadata accessor results typed at the call
    (`option swifttypes`).

    Issue: a stripped Swift image calls `sub_<addr>(cache)` before every use
    of a generic type instantiated at a concrete type, and `sub_<addr>()` for
    every conformance witness table it builds lazily; neither the helper,
    nor the type it is asked for, nor the conformance is visible. The
    helper's body (a cache test, then `swift_getTypeByMangledNameInContext2`
    on a relative mangled name) and the accessor's (a cache test, then a
    metadata call and `swift_getWitnessTable`) say what they are, and the
    mangled name the call passes says which type. On a large third-party
    iOS app the main binary has two copies of the helper with thousands of
    call sites. A generic type's metadata accessor result stays `long`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P1 external-refinement, the Swift metadata pass
    (`kuna-analysis/src/analyzers/swift/kuna_swifthelpers.rs`), and P5
    type propagation (`kuna-decomp/src/p5_types/kuna_swiftmetaresult.rs`,
    a `sitetypelock` client).

    P0 assertion: `option swifthelpers on|off` (default on; inert unless
    `swiftmetadata` is on) and `option swifttypes on|off`.

    Both passes clear the harness architecture and load the real fixture
    `swift_helpers_arm64_elf` (arm64 ELF shared object, swiftc 6.4.2
    `-parse-stdlib`, `llvm-objcopy strip-all`) with `swiftmetadata on`.
    Pass 1 (`swifthelpers off`, `swifttypes off`) reproduces the BUG:
    `hf.useBox(_:)` calls `sub_112e4(0x31da0)` and `sub_11328()`, and
    `hf.pair(_:)` keeps the accessor result in a `long`. Pass 2 (the
    defaults) is the FIX: the helper call names the cache after
    `hf.Box<hf.Unit>`, the accessor is
    `<hf.Box<hf.Unit>:hf.Shape>.lazy_protocol_witness_table_accessor`
    with its cache, and the accessor results are `swift.type_metadata *`,
    also in `hf.wrap(_:)`, which loads the value witness table below one
    (`valuewitness`).
-->
